00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:34.960
The world is simply too vast for any organism&nbsp;
to find its way alone. There are too many turns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:41.360
too many challenges...inevitably, we all reach&nbsp;
points where we need just a little bit of help.&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:48.320
And the microcosmos is no exception. The world’s&nbsp;
earliest relationship builders were microbes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:54.560
who—through some combination of chance&nbsp;
and biology—found ways to use each other.&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.680
The word for this, of course, is symbiosis,&nbsp;
and it is not restricted to microbes. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.560
can watch many a nature documentary and&nbsp;
find many examples of birds or fishes or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:10.480
large mammals that form symbiotic relationships.&nbsp;
Perhaps one feeds on the pests living on another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.720
and the other provides shelter.
But there are many symbiotic relationships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:20.160
that we can see under the microscope as well.&nbsp;
One of the most famous examples of symbiosis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:26.320
is that of Paramecium bursaria. The Paramecium&nbsp;
bursaria looks like an ellipse with hairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.880
poking out of it, which means that for the most&nbsp;
part, it looks just like any other paramecium.&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:38.080
But there’s one big distinction: the Paramecium&nbsp;
bursaria is green. And it’s green thanks to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.760
whole host of algae called Chlorella that have&nbsp;
taken up residence inside its body because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:50.640
well, generations ago, many generations ago,&nbsp;
a Paramecium ate some algae. But instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:57.920
digesting the algae, the Paramecium just didn’t.&nbsp;
And instead, it started to consume the products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:03.440
of the Chlorella’s synthesis and then it&nbsp;
passed that algae on to the next generation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.000
And in return, the Chlorella gets to use&nbsp;
the paramecium—its former predator—as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.320
a form of protection and transportation.
But scientists have separated the bursaria from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.920
it’s algal symbionts and, it turns out, both of&nbsp;
them can survive on their own. Because each of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:25.680
members of the relationship benefits, it’s called&nbsp;
mutualism, but because it is optional it’s called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:31.840
“facultative.” So the paramecium bursaria and its&nbsp;
chlorella are an example of facultative mutualism.&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.320
So, this all seems pretty&nbsp;
simple and straightforward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.440
Symbiosis is a biological exchange of&nbsp;
goods and/or services, an uncomplicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:50.000
means of partnering up to help survive.
But relationships are always easier in theory. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.320
practice, they can be much more complicated&nbsp;
and difficult to understand. And for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.680
as outsiders, it takes considerable work&nbsp;
to decipher just what kind of relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.240
microbes are forming with each other.
James, our master of microscopes, found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:10.080
this ciliate in some mud. And if you’re wondering&nbsp;
what those fuzzy rod shapes are inside of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:16.960
those are prokaryotes stuffing up the ciliate.
But besides knowing that those are prokaryotes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.480
in there, we can’t really tell much about what&nbsp;
they’re doing or what their relationship is with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.240
their ciliate host. Do those prokaryotes help the&nbsp;
ciliate survive in the low-oxygen environment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.680
mud? Are they why we can’t see any food vacuoles&nbsp;
in the ciliate? Are they even helping each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.560
at all or are they parasitizing each other?
Trying to decipher all of that just by looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:43.280
it, would be like trying to describe the complex&nbsp;
dynamics of a family just by looking at their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:49.200
portrait. So to dive deeper into what’s going on,&nbsp;
James has isolated and frozen some of these cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.400
in tiny tubes, storing them for a future day&nbsp;
when he’ll be able to study them more closely.&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.240
Now, one of the disorienting aspects&nbsp;
of symbiosis at the microbial level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:09.040
is that eukaryotic organisms are full of&nbsp;
parts that look a bit like other organisms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.000
And that is not a coincidence. In some cases,&nbsp;
things like chloroplasts and mitochondria are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.160
the product of endosymbiosis, a prokaryote that&nbsp;
got consumed so completely by its symbiotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:26.560
relationship inside another organism that it has&nbsp;
become an organelle. The process of endosymbiosis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:32.480
is complete when the endosymbiont has lost most&nbsp;
of its genes or given them over to its host.&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:38.960
And that endosymbiotic history complicates our&nbsp;
human desire to distinguish between an organelle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.440
and an organism. But fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:55.920
we do have molecular tools that&nbsp;
allow us to investigate further.&nbsp;

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:01.360
This diatom, Epithemia gibba, has had at&nbsp;
least two distinct things noted about it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:08.160
one is its unusual capacity to convert extremely&nbsp;
stable but biologically useless diatomic nitrogen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:14.800
into actually useful nitrogen compounds, we call&nbsp;
this “nitrogen fixing”. And the other thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:20.800
people have noted about it is the round bits&nbsp;
that appear like beady bubbles inside of it.&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.720
When scientists looked closer, they found that the&nbsp;
round bodies had a double membrane around them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.840
suggesting that they might be another&nbsp;
organism living inside the diatom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:37.440
So they spun the diatoms and treated them with&nbsp;
chemicals to extract these mystery parts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:44.240
study the genes inside them, ultimately finding&nbsp;
that the diatom was occupied by cyanobacteria.&nbsp;

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:51.040
These cyanobacteria, it turns out, are responsible&nbsp;
for the diatom’s ability to fix nitrogen. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:58.080
when it comes to the question of organelle versus&nbsp;
organism, well this cyanobacteria is kind of both.&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:04.640
It doesn’t appear to perform photosynthesis&nbsp;
in the diatom, its main function is to fix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:10.560
nitrogen. But to enter into that relationship&nbsp;
with its host, the cyanobacteria had to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:16.720
its own nitrogen-fixation schedule to match&nbsp;
the diatom’s daily photosynthetic activities.&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:22.320
The extent of the cyanobacteria’s adaptation to&nbsp;
its host and the fact that it is passed on from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:27.520
generation to generation of diatom suggests&nbsp;
that while those cyanobacteria might still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:34.160
be their own selves, they may be on their way&nbsp;
to becoming an organelle, fully endosymbiosed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:40.080
into a piece of Epithelia gibba.
And other mysteries remain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:46.400
even when they seem initially clear-cut.&nbsp;
This Loxodes rostrum is a rare find for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:52.960
but it is also quite distinctive because it bears&nbsp;
not one, but two different types of algae in it.&nbsp;

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:58.320
And now, that might seem like yet another&nbsp;
version of the Paramecium bursaria and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:04.080
Chlorella relationship we saw earlier. We&nbsp;
have a large ciliate occupied by algae,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:09.120
combining their respective bodies and machinery to&nbsp;
supply different forms of nutrition to each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.600
that neither would get on its own. There’s&nbsp;
nothing weird about this, right? Nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:20.720
startling, nothing extraordinarily strange.
Except that Loxodes rostrum are also full of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:27.760
pigments to avoid light because they don’t like&nbsp;
light. They practically melt when they’re exposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:32.880
to light. And that’s a problem for algae because&nbsp;
light is kind of part of their thing. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:38.400
it’s an important part of photosynthesis.
So how do they survive together?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:46.080
Why are they doing this? We don’t know.&nbsp;
There must have been some kind of compromise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:53.280
The nature of that compromise remains unknown&nbsp;
to us. Maybe they’re just like that couple who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:59.920
seem deeply incompatible, and yet somehow,&nbsp;
they need each other and they make it work.&nbsp;

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:05.040
At least, that’s how it looks from&nbsp;
the outside. But in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.960
generations of Loxodes from now, maybe&nbsp;
this relationship will look different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:12.880
And perhaps, like every other&nbsp;
relationship, it will evolve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:18.720
helping both organisms confront new challenges&nbsp;
that the world will inevitably throw at them.&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:28.880
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

